#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166677 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef73a00-12f7-5818-85a9-d8b5e2582bfe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/id #uuid "d6a54cb2-ea48-57b1-bf2d-7aac09915159" :question/math-academy-id "e-8595" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}⟨0,t,4⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}⟨0,4,-t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf B(t) = \\mathbf T(t) \\times \\mathbf N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}⟨0,t,4⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}}⟨0,4,-t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & t & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -t\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+16}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}t & 4 \\\\ 4 & -t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & t \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+16}⟨(-{t}^{2}-16),-(0-0),(0-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+16}⟨-({t}^{2}+16),0,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166678 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91ea3125-c2b7-56d0-97d8-4e54b2c01524" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Specific Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/id #uuid "b2a775f5-4a27-5cba-931f-2a45db305152" :question/math-academy-id "e-8596" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at the point $t=\\pi$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,-3,4⟩, \\\\ N(\\pi ) & =⟨1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf B(t) = \\mathbf T(t) \\times \\mathbf N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(\\pi ) & =T(\\pi )\\times N(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,-3,4⟩\\times ⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨(0-0),-(0-4),(0-(-3))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166679 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d895a2-73eb-5141-b56d-48090debf57b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent"}] :question/id #uuid "e805dcee-247b-5aa3-9856-f7bd48bd1a78" :question/math-academy-id "e-8597" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,2,\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf B(t) = \\mathbf T(t) \\times \\mathbf N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $\\mathbf N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $\\mathbf{N}(t).$ To do this, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,0,\\,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡4t+16{sin}^{2}⁡4t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{16({cos}^{2}⁡4t+{sin}^{2}⁡4t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩}{(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}})} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,2,\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t⟩\\times ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡4t & 2 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t & 2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡4t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t-0,-(-{sin}^{2}⁡4t-{cos}^{2}⁡4t),0-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t,\\,1,\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91ea3125-c2b7-56d0-97d8-4e54b2c01524" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Specific Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91c16ba7-737a-5731-a5db-87aa59f9a3bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371716} {:answer/id #uuid "49467939-6bd2-5cc8-8e66-ff9c3f272469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨0,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371717} {:answer/id #uuid "fa924ac1-afe5-5c26-94e8-b613d15e59c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371718} {:answer/id #uuid "506e1a31-362b-54d8-ab5f-3d390997cf07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371719} {:answer/id #uuid "a750a149-c913-5d57-907b-cd4df32efbaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa924ac1-afe5-5c26-94e8-b613d15e59c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371718} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b5a45dd-79b2-5d34-ace5-9dd5c9b8534a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "707bba32-6581-5fec-b305-ca37df62ff61" :question/math-academy-id "q-127626" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=2$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨0,1,2⟩, \\\\ N(2) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨0,2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(2) & =T(2)\\times N(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨0,1,2⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨(-1-4),-(0-0),(0-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-5,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d895a2-73eb-5141-b56d-48090debf57b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bba9888-420b-5177-9749-b2a2df95fa99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357850} {:answer/id #uuid "5321b4d9-3b60-50aa-86d2-67d71264b404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨2,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358469} {:answer/id #uuid "6169e31e-bcf7-5263-9af6-da6e185aa542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358599} {:answer/id #uuid "96846c65-c6eb-5c00-b01c-0fde168b7d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359068} {:answer/id #uuid "d7aee3fc-9b7d-5c2c-938d-0b42025a181c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bba9888-420b-5177-9749-b2a2df95fa99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357850} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc6b7c4a-1590-5bf4-bd80-56a591de4f1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "553afed7-053a-5b06-8cb8-cd7b8f67d1b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96959" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2\\sqrt{2},-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨({sin}^{2}⁡t-(-{cos}^{2}⁡t)),-(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-0),(-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨1,\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ef73a00-12f7-5818-85a9-d8b5e2582bfe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cb47bec-7a6f-5792-ba0d-6e5b38af9aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373430} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3b30d0-baff-5a1e-9320-438cb3a67392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373431} {:answer/id #uuid "d8339d83-7333-5325-a4b4-f881c4722e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨2\\sqrt{2},0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373432} {:answer/id #uuid "80068700-acdb-51cb-9ca8-4590c1407d03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373433} {:answer/id #uuid "1ecd40c2-7430-5053-981f-1c864376a6aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ecd40c2-7430-5053-981f-1c864376a6aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373434} :answer-field/id #uuid "171888e5-1a2e-5e52-943a-a78ce9e763ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a933436d-0584-5de5-aa04-efee8c77b6c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-99409" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩, \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,0,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,0,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t & 0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =⟨0-0,-(-{cos}^{2}⁡3t-{sin}^{2}⁡3t),0-0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91ea3125-c2b7-56d0-97d8-4e54b2c01524" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Specific Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cedcb2ec-f9cf-5b2d-97d1-73c92e6a4657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1, 2, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166467} {:answer/id #uuid "d5dc64fb-366a-50d0-b0de-982441d7d6c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2, 1, 0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166468} {:answer/id #uuid "dd03ee71-8291-5f27-8e14-186f1aff231e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨ - 1,-1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166469} {:answer/id #uuid "fd08544a-892b-5d85-961b-7deb4532cf5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨ - 1, 1, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166470} {:answer/id #uuid "3b18103a-c14d-523c-b469-c099662d1c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨ - 1,-1, 2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166471}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6706dd19-f83f-5a69-b331-b8b66eafd0da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166466}] :question/id #uuid "38d9f4a8-a08e-535a-9477-a86a4bf7e353" :question/math-academy-id "q-99414" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t = 0$, given that\n\n$T(0)|= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1, 1, 1⟩,; N(0)|= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨ - 1, 1, 0⟩$.\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91ea3125-c2b7-56d0-97d8-4e54b2c01524" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Specific Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a1ae09-acc7-534d-998d-675a3a672bfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360903} {:answer/id #uuid "36500aea-1d78-5e1e-8a4e-61225fd63f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}⟨2,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361081} {:answer/id #uuid "3c46b9db-5c5d-5ffa-a69e-c952c7bb9bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-2,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361114} {:answer/id #uuid "d23b25e5-b525-5f64-8360-2fafe7036e33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}⟨-3,5,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361776} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b49b66-cdf9-5bd2-8a0f-81369247e0e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d23b25e5-b525-5f64-8360-2fafe7036e33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}⟨-3,5,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361776} :answer-field/id #uuid "024da017-5d31-5d75-8afa-e61bb675b2be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36e9c863-f2ed-5c63-bcea-d3ee9076eeb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99415" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}⟨3,5,4⟩, \\\\ N(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,0,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =T(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\times N(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}⟨3,5,4⟩\\times \\frac{1}{5}⟨4,0,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25\\sqrt{2}}⟨(-15-0),-(-9-16),(0-20)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25\\sqrt{2}}⟨-15,25,-20⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}⟨-3,5,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91ea3125-c2b7-56d0-97d8-4e54b2c01524" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Specific Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent and Principal Normal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d3a79db-d496-5533-9506-ddba2f2e68d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360954} {:answer/id #uuid "7f642486-7c50-5f59-9a1e-b49539d68a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361405} {:answer/id #uuid "baa23e32-f72c-5329-a831-447665cb25b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361669} {:answer/id #uuid "c08bb47f-56f7-55f2-8bdb-42aeb6789b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,-4,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361698} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ab4af3-af01-5bc5-b1ca-9c503567f82c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baa23e32-f72c-5329-a831-447665cb25b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361669} :answer-field/id #uuid "22732420-8d08-537c-963c-63f5aee44079" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be578a50-bace-5ae6-a42e-46e5c22a651d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99420" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $t=1,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,2,1⟩, \\\\ N(1) & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}⟨-4,5,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(1) & =T(1)\\times N(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,2,1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}⟨-4,5,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\sqrt{5}}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\sqrt{5}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\sqrt{5}}⟨(-4-5),-(-4-(-4)),(10-(-8))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\sqrt{5}}⟨-9,0,18⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166837 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d895a2-73eb-5141-b56d-48090debf57b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the General Binormal Vector to a Curve Given the Unit Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e27e2e53-2bd4-5fd9-9c54-e7bc6e453f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-1,-2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373437} {:answer/id #uuid "fc30b76d-661e-5b7f-b41f-e7214a9e3f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373438} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1c628f-d942-52cb-aa7e-76e397624b9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,-1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373439} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec3b037-17fc-5106-a762-c7c3c5806797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨2,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373440} {:answer/id #uuid "99258556-2e23-5049-afc5-20a446995e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99258556-2e23-5049-afc5-20a446995e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373441} :answer-field/id #uuid "f18b47dd-75b2-5271-b7af-f2abcbd51dfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28b318e9-60c2-5e57-acb1-0cc92a2b71d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-99583" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨2{e}^{t},{e}^{t},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,\\frac{{e}^{t}}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{5{e}^{2t}}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨2{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t},\\,-5{e}^{2t}⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(2{e}^{t})^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}+{(-5{e}^{2t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}+25{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}{e}^{t}}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{1+5{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}{e}^{t}}{5{e}^{2t}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{(5{e}^{2t}+1)^{3/2}}⟨2{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t},\\,-5{e}^{2t}⟩}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}{e}^{t}}{5{e}^{2t}+1})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨2,\\,1,\\,-5{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨2{e}^{t},{e}^{t},1⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\sqrt{5{e}^{2t}+1}}⟨2,\\,1,\\,-5{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(5{e}^{2t}+1)}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -5{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(5{e}^{2t}+1)}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} & 1 \\\\ 2 & -5{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(5{e}^{2t}+1)}⟨-5{e}^{2t}-1,-(-10{e}^{2t}-2),2{e}^{t}-2{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}(5{e}^{2t}+1)}⟨-(5{e}^{2t}+1),\\,2(5{e}^{2t}+1),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨-1,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
